            {
              "id": "7bb2f645-8462-3a36-a0a4-f5dcd6111b32",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA \"Vulnerable Hash Algorithm\" vulnerability refers to the use of weak or broken cryptographic algorithms for hashing sensitive data. In the context of C++, this vulnerability can occur when functions from outdated or insecure libraries, such as MD5, are used for hashing. MD5 is considered insecure as it is susceptible to hash collisions, where different inputs produce the same hash output, making it easier for attackers to reverse-engineer the hashed data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use a stronger, more secure hashing algorithm such as SHA-256 or SHA-3. These algorithms are currently considered secure and are less likely to suffer from hash collisions. Additionally, consider using a salt (random data) when hashing to further increase security.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the MD5_Init function with a function from a more secure library. For example, you could use the SHA256_Init function from the OpenSSL library:\n\n```cpp\n#include <openssl/sha.h>\n\nSHA256_CTX ctx;\nSHA256_Init(&ctx);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the OpenSSL library to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS126858"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "621ae85d-be14-3380-b908-217fe5945119",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nVulnerable Hash Algorithm vulnerability refers to the use of weak or broken cryptographic algorithms for hashing sensitive data. In the context of C++, this vulnerability can occur when using outdated or insecure libraries for cryptographic operations. The MD5 algorithm, for example, is considered insecure and vulnerable to collision attacks, where two different inputs produce the same hash output.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use a stronger, more secure hash algorithm such as SHA-256 or SHA-3. These algorithms are currently considered secure and are not known to be vulnerable to collision attacks. Additionally, it is important to keep cryptographic libraries up to date to ensure the latest security patches and improvements are applied.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the MD5_Final function with a function that uses a more secure hash algorithm. Here is an example using the OpenSSL library's SHA256 function:\n\n```cpp\n#include <openssl/sha.h>\n\nSHA256_CTX ctx;\nSHA256_Init(&ctx);\nSHA256_Update(&ctx, data, data_length);\nSHA256_Final(result.data(), &ctx);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the OpenSSL library to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are valid and active at the time of writing. They should be checked regularly to ensure they remain accessible to users."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS126858"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d0c8c318-a402-3a86-aaad-c3403203409d",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA \"Vulnerable Hash Algorithm\" vulnerability refers to the use of weak or broken cryptographic algorithms for hashing sensitive data. In the context of C++ programming, this vulnerability can occur when a developer uses a weak cryptographic hash function like MD5. MD5 is considered insecure for many cryptographic functions as it is susceptible to hash collisions, where two different inputs produce the same hash output, making it easier for an attacker to reverse-engineer the hashed value.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use a stronger, more secure hash function such as SHA-256 or SHA-3. These algorithms are currently considered secure and are not known to have any collision vulnerabilities. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the MD5 hash function with a more secure hash function. Here is an example of how to use the SHA-256 hash function with the OpenSSL library in C++:\n\n```cpp\n#include <openssl/sha.h>\n\nSHA256_CTX ctx;\nSHA256_Init(&ctx);\nSHA256_Update(&ctx, data, length);\nunsigned char hash[SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH];\nSHA256_Final(hash, &ctx);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe OpenSSL library is required for the SHA-256 hash function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS126858"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "8cdf8037-d6fb-3e30-aeff-10f43e9b47fc",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA \"Vulnerable Hash Algorithm\" vulnerability refers to the use of weak or broken cryptographic algorithms for hashing data. In the context of C++, this vulnerability can occur when functions from outdated or insecure libraries, such as MD5, are used for hashing. MD5 is considered insecure as it is susceptible to hash collisions, where different inputs produce the same hash output, making it easier for attackers to reverse-engineer the hashed data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use a stronger, more secure hashing algorithm such as SHA-256 or SHA-3. These algorithms are currently considered secure and are not susceptible to the same vulnerabilities as MD5.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the MD5_Update function with a function from a more secure library. Here is an example using the OpenSSL library's SHA256 function:\n\n```cpp\n#include <openssl/sha.h>\n\nSHA256_CTX ctx;\nSHA256_Init(&ctx);\nSHA256_Update(&ctx, s.data(), s.length());\nunsigned char result[SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH];\nSHA256_Final(result, &ctx);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe original code requires the OpenSSL library. The fixed code also requires the OpenSSL library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS126858"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "1f2b0b91-3baf-391f-9c25-c9f7a83e7359",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA \"Vulnerable Hash Algorithm\" vulnerability in C++ programming language refers to the use of weak or broken cryptographic hash functions. Cryptographic hash functions are mathematical operations run on digital data; by comparing the computed hash (the output from execution of the algorithm) to a known and expected hash value, a person can determine the data's integrity. A weak hash algorithm can lead to situations where an attacker can easily generate a different input with the same hash output, leading to potential security risks such as password cracking, data integrity issues, and other forms of data manipulation.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use strong, industry-accepted cryptographic algorithms. Avoid using weak algorithms such as MD5 or SHA1, which have known vulnerabilities. Instead, use stronger algorithms like SHA-256 or SHA-3. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe source code fix would depend on the specific hash function being used. However, if you were using a weak hash function like MD5, you could replace it with a stronger one like SHA-256. Here's an example of how you might do this using the OpenSSL library in C++:\n\n```cpp\n#include <openssl/sha.h>\n\n// ...\n\nunsigned char digest[SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH];\nSHA256((unsigned char*)&data, sizeof(data), (unsigned char*)&digest); \n\nchar mdString[SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH*2+1];\nfor (int i = 0; i < SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH; i++)\n    sprintf(&mdString[i*2], \"%02x\", (unsigned int)digest[i]);\n\nprintf(\"SHA256 digest: %s\\n\", mdString);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe OpenSSL library is required for the above code example to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS126858"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "532491a3-8ea8-37b4-b4e1-60290af9309a",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA \"Vulnerable Hash Algorithm\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of weak or broken cryptographic hash functions, such as MD5. MD5 is considered to be a weak hash function as it is susceptible to hash collisions, where two different inputs produce the same hash output. This can lead to various security issues, such as password cracking, data integrity issues, and other forms of data tampering.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use a stronger, more secure hash function, such as SHA-256 or SHA-3. These hash functions are currently considered to be secure and are not susceptible to hash collisions like MD5.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is an example of how to replace MD5 with SHA-256 using the OpenSSL library in C++:\n\n```cpp\n#include <openssl/sha.h>\n#include <string>\n#include <iostream>\n\nstd::string sha256(const std::string str)\n{\n    unsigned char hash[SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH];\n    SHA256_CTX sha256;\n    SHA256_Init(&sha256);\n    SHA256_Update(&sha256, str.c_str(), str.size());\n    SHA256_Final(hash, &sha256);\n    std::stringstream ss;\n    for(int i = 0; i < SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH; i++)\n    {\n        ss << std::hex << std::setw(2) << std::setfill('0') << (int)hash[i];\n    }\n    return ss.str();\n}\n\nint main() {\n    std::string input = \"hello\";\n    std::string output = sha256(input);\n    std::cout << output << std::endl;\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe above code requires the OpenSSL library. You can install it using the following command:\n\n```bash\nsudo apt-get install libssl-dev\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS126858",
                  "532491a3-8ea8-37b4-b4e1-60290af9309a"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f97ed3a2-f0f9-3a37-86b4-770e1c9cc24c",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA \"Vulnerable Hash Algorithm\" vulnerability refers to the use of weak or broken cryptographic hash functions in a software application. In C++ programming, this vulnerability can occur when a developer uses a weak hash function like MD5. MD5 is considered weak because it is susceptible to collision attacks, where two different inputs produce the same hash output, thus compromising the integrity of the data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use stronger hash functions such as SHA-256 or SHA-3. These hash functions are currently considered secure and are not susceptible to known collision attacks. Additionally, it is important to keep up to date with the latest cryptographic research and standards, as the security of hash functions can change over time.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the use of MD5 with a stronger hash function. Here is an example of how to use SHA-256 with the OpenSSL library in C++:\n\n```cpp\n#include <openssl/sha.h>\n\nstd::string sha256(const std::string str)\n{\n    unsigned char hash[SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH];\n    SHA256_CTX sha256;\n    SHA256_Init(&sha256);\n    SHA256_Update(&sha256, str.c_str(), str.size());\n    SHA256_Final(hash, &sha256);\n    std::stringstream ss;\n    for(int i = 0; i < SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH; i++)\n    {\n        ss << std::hex << std::setw(2) << std::setfill('0') << (int)hash[i];\n    }\n    return ss.str();\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe above code example requires the OpenSSL library. You can install it on Ubuntu with the following command:\n\n```bash\nsudo apt-get install libssl-dev\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS126858"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "1d4f286f-0904-3f31-9ce5-0a426e4e95e5",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis detected a possible undefined behavior risk due to overlapping memory regions between the source and destination buffers in a memory copy operation. The destination and source may alias, as indicated by an assignment involving the same data pointer. Additionally, there is no evidence of a guard or check to prevent this overlap. The source may also be null, as there is no prior check ensuring it is non-null. These factors increase the likelihood of undefined behavior, which can lead to unpredictable program behavior or security vulnerabilities. The source is not a string literal or constant, so the overlap risk is not mitigated by intrinsic properties of the data.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory where the source and destination regions may overlap, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`. `memmove` is specifically designed to handle overlapping memory regions safely. Replace the original call with:\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(ctx->buffer, data, size);\n```\n\nThis change ensures that the copy operation is safe even if the source and destination buffers overlap.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP: Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "0ef7f0a1-8098-3cd1-982e-867203bfcf8f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the analysis detected a possible undefined behavior risk due to overlapping memory regions between the source and destination in a memory copy operation. The dataflow analysis indicates that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory, which can cause undefined behavior with functions like memcpy. Additionally, there is no evidence of a guard or check to prevent this overlap, and the source may also be null, which further increases the risk. The destination appears to be a buffer within a structure, and the source is an identifier, making overlap feasible. These factors collectively indicate a real risk of undefined behavior and potential security issues.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap. If there is any possibility of overlap, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`, as `memmove` is designed to handle overlapping regions safely. For example, replace the original call with:\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(&ctx->buffer[used], data, available);\n```\n\nAdditionally, consider adding explicit checks to ensure that `data` is not null before performing the copy to avoid null pointer dereference issues.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a88520c1-7891-363e-bf30-9fb43673fc71",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA \"Vulnerable Hash Algorithm\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of weak or broken cryptographic hash functions, such as MD5. MD5 is considered to be a weak hash function as it is susceptible to hash collisions, where two different inputs produce the same hash output. This can lead to various security issues, such as password cracking, data integrity issues, and other forms of data tampering.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use a stronger, more secure hash function, such as SHA-256 or SHA-3. These hash functions are currently considered to be secure and are not susceptible to hash collisions like MD5.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is an example of how to replace MD5 with SHA-256 using the OpenSSL library in C++:\n\n```cpp\n#include <openssl/sha.h>\n#include <string>\n#include <iostream>\n\nstd::string sha256(const std::string str)\n{\n    unsigned char hash[SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH];\n    SHA256_CTX sha256;\n    SHA256_Init(&sha256);\n    SHA256_Update(&sha256, str.c_str(), str.size());\n    SHA256_Final(hash, &sha256);\n    std::stringstream ss;\n    for(int i = 0; i < SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH; i++)\n    {\n        ss << std::hex << std::setw(2) << std::setfill('0') << (int)hash[i];\n    }\n    return ss.str();\n}\n\nint main() {\n    std::string input = \"hello\";\n    std::string output = sha256(input);\n    std::cout << output << std::endl;\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe above code requires the OpenSSL library. You can install it using the following command:\n\n```bash\nsudo apt-get install libssl-dev\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS126858",
                  "a88520c1-7891-363e-bf30-9fb43673fc71"
