              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA \"Vulnerable Hash Algorithm\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of weak or broken cryptographic hash functions, such as MD5. MD5 is considered to be a weak hash function as it is susceptible to hash collisions, where two different inputs produce the same hash output. This can lead to various security issues, such as password cracking, data integrity issues, and other forms of data tampering.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use a stronger, more secure hash function, such as SHA-256 or SHA-3. These hash functions are currently considered to be secure and are not susceptible to hash collisions like MD5.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is an example of how to replace MD5 with SHA-256 using the OpenSSL library in C++:\n\n```cpp\n#include <openssl/sha.h>\n#include <string>\n#include <iostream>\n\nstd::string sha256(const std::string str)\n{\n    unsigned char hash[SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH];\n    SHA256_CTX sha256;\n    SHA256_Init(&sha256);\n    SHA256_Update(&sha256, str.c_str(), str.size());\n    SHA256_Final(hash, &sha256);\n    std::stringstream ss;\n    for(int i = 0; i < SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH; i++)\n    {\n        ss << std::hex << std::setw(2) << std::setfill('0') << (int)hash[i];\n    }\n    return ss.str();\n}\n\nint main() {\n    std::string input = \"hello\";\n    std::string output = sha256(input);\n    std::cout << output << std::endl;\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe above code requires the OpenSSL library. You can install it using the following command:\n\n```bash\nsudo apt-get install libssl-dev\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)"
