              "id": "82cd63e4-d340-3a25-91cd-888b50742768",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Implementation of Vulnerable Cryptography Hash Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of weak or broken cryptographic hash functions in a C++ program. Cryptographic hash functions are used to convert input data into a fixed size hash value, which is typically used for password storage, data integrity checks, and digital signatures. If a weak or broken hash function is used, it can lead to various security issues such as password cracking, data tampering, and spoofing.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the `STEP` function is a part of a cryptographic hash function. If this function or the hash function it is a part of is known to be weak or broken, it can lead to the aforementioned security issues.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use strong and well-vetted cryptographic hash functions such as SHA-256 or SHA-3. Avoid using deprecated or broken hash functions such as MD5 or SHA-1.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nWithout the full context of the `STEP` function and the hash function it is a part of, it's hard to provide a specific fix. However, if the `STEP` function is a part of a weak hash function such as MD5, it would be recommended to replace the entire hash function with a stronger one such as SHA-256.\n\n```cpp\n#include <openssl/sha.h>\n\n// ...\n\nunsigned char digest[SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH];\nSHA256((unsigned char*)&data, sizeof(data), (unsigned char*)&digest); \n\nchar mdString[SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH*2+1];\nfor (int i = 0; i < SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH; i++)\n    sprintf(&mdString[i*2], \"%02x\", (unsigned int)digest[i]);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe OpenSSL library is required for the SHA-256 hash function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)"
