            {
              "id": "3ff0d962-eebe-34b1-8ac9-e689b2a268bf",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA \"Vulnerable Hash Algorithm\" vulnerability in C++ programming language refers to the use of weak or broken cryptographic algorithms for hashing. This can lead to a variety of security issues, such as the ability for an attacker to reverse the hash or create collisions, potentially leading to unauthorized access or information disclosure. In your code, you are using MD5, which is considered to be a weak hash function due to its vulnerability to collision attacks.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should replace the weak hash function with a stronger one. Hash functions such as SHA-256 or SHA-3 are currently recommended. It's also important to keep up to date with the latest cryptographic recommendations, as the strength of hash functions can change over time as new attacks are discovered.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the MD5 hash function with a stronger one, such as SHA-256. Here is an example of how to do this using the OpenSSL library:\n\n```cpp\n#include <openssl/sha.h>\n\nunsigned char block[SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH];\nSHA256((unsigned char*)&data, sizeof(data), (unsigned char*)&block);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe OpenSSL library is required for the code example to execute properly.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [Password Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Password_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)"
