            {
              "id": "66024b3f-7bc5-33fb-8e8d-0441b19ba2c0",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the use of the `strlen()` function in C++. The `strlen()` function is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if the string is not null-terminated, as `strlen()` counts characters until it reaches a null character. If a null character is not found, `strlen()` could continue reading and return an incorrect length, potentially leading to buffer overflows, memory corruption, and other issues.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `strlen(keys[i])` is used as the limit in the `strncmp()` function. If `keys[i]` is not null-terminated, `strlen()` could return an incorrect length, leading to unexpected behavior in `strncmp()`.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, ensure that all strings are null-terminated before passing them to `strlen()`. Additionally, consider using safer alternatives to `strlen()`, such as `strnlen()`, which takes a maximum length as an argument and will not read past this limit, even if a null character is not found.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t len = strnlen(keys[i], MAX_LENGTH);\nif (strncmp(p, keys[i], len) == 0) {\n    // ...\n}\n```\n\nIn this fix, `strnlen()` is used instead of `strlen()`. `MAX_LENGTH` should be defined as the maximum expected length of `keys[i]`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstring>`: This library provides the `strlen()` and `strncmp()` functions.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are subject to change and may not be accessible in the future."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "8ab23c78-85e1-314b-a958-7a6c4c5728e5",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcat` function in C++ is used to concatenate strings. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not perform bounds checking, and thus can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities. If the destination buffer is not large enough to contain the concatenated result, this can lead to overwriting of adjacent memory, leading to unpredictable program behavior, crashes, and potential code execution vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer string handling functions that perform bounds checking, such as `strncat`, `strlcat`, or consider using higher-level string abstractions provided by C++, such as `std::string`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcat` function with `strncat` function, which includes the size of the destination buffer as a parameter:\n\n```cpp\nstrncat(temp, imageNames[j], sizeof(temp) - strlen(temp) - 1);\n```\n\nThis ensures that the concatenation does not exceed the size of the destination buffer, preventing buffer overflow.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<cstring>` library for the `strcat` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS141863"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "90e986ec-a1e9-38a7-a9e7-bf1220b32e41",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnsecured Web Link Vulnerability in C++ is not a direct vulnerability in the language itself, but rather a potential security risk that can occur when a C++ application interacts with web resources. This vulnerability arises when a C++ application uses an unsecured (non-HTTPS) link to access web resources. This can expose the application to various attacks such as Man-in-the-Middle (MitM) attacks, where an attacker can intercept and potentially alter the data being transmitted.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use secure HTTPS links when your application needs to access web resources. HTTPS encrypts the data being transmitted, making it much more difficult for an attacker to intercept or alter it.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIf you're using a library like libcurl to handle HTTP requests, you can simply replace the unsecured \"http://\" prefix with \"https://\". For example, if the vulnerable code is:\n\n```cpp\nCURL *curl = curl_easy_init();\nif(curl) {\n  CURLcode res;\n  curl_easy_setopt(curl, CURLOPT_URL, \"http://www.apache.org\");\n  /* Perform the request, res will get the return code */ \n  res = curl_easy_perform(curl);\n  /* Check for errors */ \n  if(res != CURLE_OK)\n    fprintf(stderr, \"curl_easy_perform() failed: %s\\n\",\n            curl_easy_strerror(res));\n  /* always cleanup */ \n  curl_easy_cleanup(curl);\n}\n```\n\nYou can fix the vulnerability by changing the URL to use HTTPS:\n\n```cpp\nCURL *curl = curl_easy_init();\nif(curl) {\n  CURLcode res;\n  curl_easy_setopt(curl, CURLOPT_URL, \"https://www.apache.org\");\n  /* Perform the request, res will get the return code */ \n  res = curl_easy_perform(curl);\n  /* Check for errors */ \n  if(res != CURLE_OK)\n    fprintf(stderr, \"curl_easy_perform() failed: %s\\n\",\n            curl_easy_strerror(res));\n  /* always cleanup */ \n  curl_easy_cleanup(curl);\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the libcurl library.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS137138"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "3ab1a4b3-1ec6-33e0-94c8-aa84f78ba5f5",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string. It is considered unsafe because it does not check the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow if the source string is larger than the destination buffer. This can cause a program to crash or, in the worst case, allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer string copy functions that check the size of the destination buffer. These include `strncpy`, `strlcpy`, or `memcpy`. However, these functions can also be misused, so it's important to ensure that the size argument correctly represents the size of the destination buffer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcpy` function with a safer function, such as `strncpy`. Here's how you can do it:\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(temp, dst, sizeof(temp));\ntemp[sizeof(temp) - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strcpy` and `strncpy` functions are part of the C standard library, so you need to include the `cstring` header:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS185832"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "75c3f5d4-8565-38f3-a1db-e4574b246075",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the code copies memory from one buffer to another using a standard memory copy function, but there are no checks to ensure that the source buffer is not null. If the source pointer is null at runtime, this will result in undefined behavior, potentially causing a crash or other unpredictable results. The analysis did not detect any prior validation or guard that would guarantee the source pointer is valid before the copy operation. The destination buffer is likely safe, but the risk remains due to the unchecked source pointer.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nBefore performing the memory copy, ensure that the source pointer is not null. Add an explicit check to prevent undefined behavior if the source is invalid.\n\n```cpp\nif (output_row != nullptr) {\n    memcpy(temp_output_row, output_row, elementsInRow * sizeof(Rpp8u));\n} else {\n    // Handle error: source buffer is null\n    // For example, log an error or return an error code\n}\n```\n\nThis check ensures that the memory copy only occurs when the source buffer is valid, preventing crashes or unpredictable behavior.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "07c032d8-5c27-34bf-83aa-a8562ad2564e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcat` function in C++ is used to concatenate strings. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not perform bounds checking, and thus can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities. If the destination buffer is not large enough to contain the concatenated result, this can lead to overwriting of adjacent memory, leading to unpredictable program behavior, crashes, and potential code execution.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer string handling functions that perform bounds checking, such as `strncat`, `strlcat`, or `snprintf`. Always ensure that your buffers are large enough to contain any data that will be written to them, including null terminators.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcat` function with a safer alternative. For example, you could use `strncat` like this:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n\n// ...\n\nchar dst[256];\nstrncat(dst, \"/\", sizeof(dst) - strlen(dst) - 1);\n```\n\nThis code will ensure that no more than `sizeof(dst) - strlen(dst) - 1` characters are written to `dst`, preventing buffer overflow.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<cstring>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS141863"
                ]
              }
            }
          ],
          "language": "en-US",
          "contents": [
            "localizedData",
            "nonLocalizedData"
          ],
          "isComprehensive": false
        }
